# The sparse mirror: every dense vector representation has an SAE twin.
#
#   sae_last            <-> last_token          code of the step's final token
#   sae_mean            <-> step_mean           mean of the codes of its tokens
#   sae_delta           <-> step_delta          code(last) - code(boundary)
#   sae_stats           <-> step_stats          concat[mean,max,min,std,last]
#   sae_boundary_stats  <-> boundary_stats      boundary code prepended
#
# Same layer, same activations, same trainer, same hyperparameter protocol as the
# dense grid, so the only thing that changes is whether the learner reads the raw
# 4,096-dim state or its 65,536-wide sparse code. Qwen-Scope TopK, k=50,
# reconstruction FVU 0.336 at this layer.
#
# READ THE LINEAR COLUMN FOR THE SPARSITY CLAIM. A linear probe is dim+1
# parameters, so 65,537 against 4,097 -- both negligible, no capacity story
# needed. The MLP columns are NOT capacity-matched to their dense twins:
# sae_stats x mlp:h1024 is 336M parameters against step_stats x mlp:h1024's 21M,
# a 16x gap that would flatter any SAE "win". They are here for the capacity
# curve, not for the headline.
#
# Prediction on record: these should lose. Reconstruction discards a third of the
# variance, and section 15 found step correctness to be a distributed
# low-variance margin, which is what a dictionary trained for reconstruction has
# no reason to preserve.

sae_last linear
sae_last mlp:h1024
sae_mean linear
sae_mean mlp:h1024
sae_delta linear
sae_delta mlp:h1024
sae_stats linear
sae_stats mlp:h1024
sae_boundary_stats linear
sae_boundary_stats mlp:h1024
